    mainDescription:
      "<p>Form 8824 preparation compiles the data and documentation Denver, Colorado investors need to support their CPA's completion of IRS Form 8824, the form required to report a Section 1031 like kind exchange on the investor's federal tax return for the year the relinquished property sold. Form 8824 requires detailed information about both the relinquished and replacement property, the dates of transfer and identification, the fair market values and adjusted basis of each property, any liabilities assumed or relieved, and the calculation of realized gain, recognized gain, and basis in the replacement property.</p><p>We compile the underlying transaction data throughout the exchange process rather than waiting until tax season, including closing statements from both the relinquished and replacement property transactions, the identification letter and its delivery date, the qualified intermediary's exchange agreement and final accounting statement, and any documentation of boot received or debt assumed. Having this documentation organized as the exchange progresses, rather than reconstructed months later from memory and scattered files, significantly reduces the risk of errors or omissions when Form 8824 is prepared.</p><h3>What the form requires and why timing matters</h3><p>Form 8824 asks for specific dates, including when the relinquished property was originally acquired, when it was transferred in the exchange, when the replacement property was identified, and when the replacement property was received, since these dates are what the IRS uses to confirm the forty five day and one hundred eighty day deadlines were satisfied. We organize this date documentation clearly, cross referenced against the identification letter and closing statements, so the investor's CPA has a complete and internally consistent record to work from rather than needing to track down missing dates during return preparation.</p><p>The form also requires calculation of realized gain, which is the total economic gain on the relinquished property before any deferral, and recognized gain, which is the taxable portion attributable to boot received. We compile the inputs for both calculations, including sale price, selling expenses, adjusted basis, and any boot received during the exchange, though the final gain calculation and its entry on the form remains the CPA's responsibility as the preparer of the investor's tax return.</p><h3>Coordinating a clean handoff to the CPA</h3><p>Because Form 8824 errors or omissions can trigger IRS correspondence or, in more serious cases, disqualify claimed deferral if the exchange cannot be adequately substantiated, we organize the compiled documentation into a clear package for handoff to the investor's CPA well before the tax filing deadline, rather than delivering it in a rushed, disorganized batch close to the deadline. This includes flagging any documentation gaps early enough that missing items, such as a closing statement the title company has not yet delivered, can be tracked down before filing rather than after.</p><p>A Section 1031 exchange defers, rather than eliminates, capital gains and depreciation recapture tax, and Form 8824 is the mechanism by which that deferral is formally claimed and documented with the IRS. This service compiles data and documentation to support the investor's CPA and does not constitute tax advice or tax return preparation; Denver, Colorado investors should have Form 8824 completed and filed by a licensed CPA or tax preparer.</p><p>Colorado generally follows the federal Form 8824 reporting for state income tax purposes, meaning deferred gain reported federally is not separately taxed at the state level, but any recognized gain from boot or a partial exchange flows through to the investor's Colorado return and is taxed at the state's flat rate alongside ordinary income. We make sure the documentation compiled for federal Form 8824 preparation is equally usable for Colorado state filing, so the investor's CPA is not reconciling two separate sets of figures for what should be a consistent transaction record.</p><p>We also compile documentation for any prior exchanges the investor has completed on the same lineage of properties, since a property that has already passed through one or more prior exchanges carries basis history that affects the current year's Form 8824 calculation, and this multi-generation basis tracking is one of the more error-prone areas of exchange reporting without complete historical records.</p><p>We also confirm the treatment of any exchange involving more than one relinquished or replacement property, since multi-property exchanges require additional detail on Form 8824 to allocate basis and gain correctly across each property, and this allocation is one of the areas where incomplete documentation most commonly leads to preparation delays or errors.</p>",
